    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Interpretation – Investments vs FinEdu & Digital:**\n",
        "\n",
        "There is a clear monotonic gradient: clients investing in PAC (capital accumulation) plans have the highest median financial education (0.538 vs 0.469 for non-investors) and digital propensity (0.555 vs 0.500). The boxplots clearly show that not only the medians, but the entire interquartile ranges (the boxes representing the middle 50% of the data) shift consistently upwards for PAC investors compared to non-investors. \n",
        "\n",
        "This makes intuitive sense: PAC plans require understanding of long-term investment horizons, and often digital platforms to manage regular payments. Lump-sum investors sit in between — they have enough financial knowledge to invest, but perhaps prefer a simpler, one-time approach.\n",
        "\n",
        "*Conclusion*: Financial education and digital propensity are key drivers (or at least strong correlates) of investment sophistication. These variables will likely differentiate clusters.\n"
      ]
