      "source": [
        "# ── Compute Gower distance matrix ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n",
        "# We pass the ORIGINAL dataframe (not one-hot encoded) so gower can detect\n",
        "# which columns are categorical and apply the right sub-distance.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Build a clean dataframe with correct dtypes for gower\n",
        "df_gower = df.copy()\n",
        "for col in categorical_columns:\n",
        "    df_gower[col] = df_gower[col].astype('object')  # gower treats object as categorical\n",
        "\n",
        "print('⏳ Computing Gower distance matrix (5000x5000) — this may take ~1-2 min...')\n",
        "gower_distances = gower.gower_matrix(df_gower)\n",
        "print(f'✅ Done. Distance matrix shape: {gower_distances.shape}')\n",
        "print(f'   Min distance: {gower_distances[gower_distances > 0].min():.4f}')\n",
        "print(f'   Max distance: {gower_distances.max():.4f}')\n",
        "print(f'   Mean distance: {gower_distances.mean():.4f}')"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        " ── Gower Distance Matrix ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n",
        "\n",
        " Gower distance is chosen because the dataset contains mixed-type variables\n",
        "  (numerical, ordinal, nominal). Unlike Euclidean distance (used by K-Means),\n",
        " Gower applies a different sub-distance for each variable type:\n",
        "\n",
        "   • Numerical   → Normalised Manhattan distance (absolute deviation)\n",
        "                   d(i,j) = |x_i - x_j| / range    → always in [0,1]\n",
        "\n",
        "   • Ordinal     → Kruskal distance (rank-based)\n",
        "                   d(i,j) = |r_i - r_j| / (L-1)    → preserves ordering\n",
        "\n",
        "   • Nominal     → Hamming distance (match/mismatch)\n",
        "                   d(i,j) = 0 if equal, 1 otherwise → no ordering assumed\n",
        "\n",
        " The final distance is the weighted average of sub-distances → always in [0,1]\n",
        "\n",
        " Why not K-Means + OHE?\n",
        " K-Means uses the arithmetic mean as centroid: for categorical variables\n",
        " the mean is meaningless (e.g. mean Job = 3.67 corresponds to no real\n",
        " category). OHE partially solves this but inflates dimensionality and\n",
        " implicitly assumes equal distances between all category pairs.\n",
        "\n",
        " Output: symmetric matrix (5000 x 5000), cell [i][j] = Gower(i,j)\n",
        " ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "jI8xlVJMZee4"
      },
      "source": [
